Script Overview
The provided Bash script is designed to perform a series of security-related tasks and system 
checks. Organization’s Threat Hunting Team (THT) publishes a daily list of Indicators of 
Compromise (IoC) on their internal system. This script can search for these IoC’s along with 
undertaking other related security checks on the organizations Ubuntu Servers. These are 
required to run with no interaction or human input.

The purpose of your script is to execute checks using this IOC file as defined above, along with 
generating some logs and a report. The outputs of the script are to be uploaded to a central 
server on completion. The script will be run via crontab between 2 and 3am every day.

Below is an overview of the script's logic and flow:

Script Initialization: The script can run either from command prompt or from Crontab. Sudo 
is needed to run script from command prompt. The script starts by changing its working 
directory to /opt/security/working.

Error Handling: The script defines paths for error log files and archives to capture potential 
issues during execution.

IOC File Download and Validation: The script downloads IOC (Indicator of Compromise) files 
and their corresponding GPG (GNU Privacy Guard) signatures from a specified URL. GPG is 
used to verify the integrity of the downloaded IOC file.

IOC File Processing: The script reads the IOC file line by line, interprets commands, and 
performs corresponding actions. Validations include file hash checks, string matches, and 
handling invalid lines.

System Checks: The script performs various system checks, such as identifying listening ports, 
firewall rules, and verifying installed packages. It reports files created or modified in the last 
48 hours, SUID (Set User ID) files, and SGID (Set Group ID) files.

Executable File Permissions: The script identifies executable files in specified directories and 
updates their permissions to remove execute permissions.

System Configuration Report: The script captures system configuration for mounted file 
systems using the mount command and saves the report to a file.

File Archiving: The script archives error logs, listening ports, firewall rules, and system 
configuration reports into a single compressed file.

File Upload and Verification: The compressed file is signed using GPG and then uploaded to 
a remote server using “Rsync”. The script validates the uploaded file's GPG signature on the 
remote server.
Final Output: The script generates a final output that includes the name, size, and SHA256 
hash of the uploaded file.

Validation and Errors: Script verify the files using pgp signature at many steps, if it can not 
verify the signature then exits and does the cleanup. If any errors encountered, then also 
script exits and does the cleanup after displaying the message in console.

Cleanup: The script performs cleanup tasks, removing temporary directories and files created 
during execution.

Installation Instructions 
=========================
Prerequisites:
1. Ubuntu: Ensure that the host system to run this script has operating system ubuntu 
22.04
2. GPG: The script uses GPG for signature verification. Install GPG using the package 
manager (apt, yum, or equivalent) for your Linux distribution. All required gpg keys 
should already be imported in host server and remote server. It is must to have gpg 
key for tht2023@tht.noroff.no.
3. Wget and IoC: Install wget to facilitate file downloads. Use the package manager to 
install these tools. The url from where IoC file is to be downloaded should be accessible 
from host server and its should have https link.
4. Sudo Permissions: Ensure that the user running the script has appropriate sudo 
permissions. The script has many commands that need sudo permission.
5. SSH Key Pair: Generate an SSH key pair using ssh-keygen if not already created. The 
public key needs to be copied to the remote server for secure file transfer.
6. Remote server login: To upload data to remote server ssh id should already be copied 
in this location /opt/security/useridentity.id, remote system should be accessible from 
host server by this id using ssh.
7. Executable: To make this script executable, save it as a file with a .sh extension (e.g.,
mkdir.sh) and then run the following command:
sudo chmod +x tth-kamsha68895.sh

How to execute:

1. Running using Crontab: Crontab is a time-based job scheduling utility in Ubuntu
operating systems. It allows users to schedule tasks (commands or scripts) to run 
periodically at fixed intervals. Crontab uses a configuration file called a "crontab file" 
to manage these scheduled tasks.

Install cron if it does not exist -
sudo apt install cron

Enable cron
sudo systemctl enable cron

Now open crontab file for editing 
sudo crontab -e

This will open the crontab file in your default text editor. Add the following line at the 
end of file to schedule your script to run daily at 2 AM:
0 2 * * * sudo /opt/security/thtreathuntingscript.sh https://int.iocserver./tth 
logs.tth.loc.org uploader123
• Here https://int.iocserver./tth is the https url from where script will 
download ioc file and its detached signature file.
• logs.tth.loc.org is the remote server to upload generated tgz file and its 
detached signature.
• uploader123 is the user identity to be used to access the remote server
Save and close crontab.
When running a script with sudo from cron, you might encounter a situation where it 
asks for a password. To avoid this, you can configure sudo to not prompt for a 
password.
Open the sudoers file for editing using the visudo command.
sudo visudo
Add the following line at the end of the sudoers file, replacing username with the 
actual username:
username ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /bin/bash /opt/security/tth-kamsha68895.sh
This line allows the specified user to run the specified script without entering a 
password. Now save and close it.
